Keying simulations
==================

Runs of the iambic keyer, one plot each. Eleven sets are kept here, five
of them at 30 WPM and six at 40, and paddle_sweep.py can produce more at
any speed.

Each plot has three panels against one time axis, zeroed on the paddle
that closes first:

  - what the operator did: the two paddles' closures
  - what the operator heard: the sidetone as rendered, each tone named
    and carrying the length it actually came out at -- the tone by
    itself, and not the mix it reaches them in
  - what the K4 was sent: every KZD and KZU, key down above the line
    and key up below

That third panel is the key commands alone. A burst also mutes the K4's
own sidetone monitor as it begins, with ML0000, and puts it back when
it ends, so that the K4's sidetone is not heard against the one the app
renders. Neither is a key edge, and drawn with the rest the mute would
hang below the line looking like a key up. The summaries record what
was sent, so they do carry it.

Every tone comes out at exactly the length its KZU carries -- 40ms or
120ms at 30 WPM, 30ms or 90ms at 40 -- across every scenario and every
run of them. The sidetone catch-up shortens tones a fraction where the
audio has drifted past the lag the keyer is working to, and over the
device these were run against it never has: the delay sits at what was
measured of it. A device that drifts would show the tones a shade
under, and the K4 still told the full length, so nothing on the air is
ever short.


keying_30wpm_swap_*.png -- twenty-one runs
------------------------------------------

The dit paddle closes at time zero, and at the moment named in the
file's name the operator releases the dit paddle and closes the dah,
holding it. That moment moves 4ms per run, from 84ms to 164ms.

What they are here to show is that once the first dah has gone out, no
dit follows it. Across the sweep the only thing that varies is how many
dits precede the switch, which is what should vary:

     84-104ms   dit dah dah dah
    108-164ms   dit dit dah dah dah

Before the dit up memory was made to require that the paddle had also
been closed during the element it is remembered from, 13 of these 21
runs put an unasked-for dit after the first dah -- as element 3 below
108ms and as element 4 from 140ms up.

Summary: summary_30wpm_swap.json


keying_30wpm_dit-tap_*.png -- fifty-six runs
--------------------------------------------

The mirror of the dah tap below: the dah paddle closes at time zero and
is held for the whole run, and the dit paddle is tapped for 5ms at the
moment named in the file's name. That moment moves 4ms per run, from
80ms to 300ms -- nearly five dah element periods, a dah being three
times a dit.

Every run inserts exactly one dit into the stream of dahs, never two
and never none, and the dahs resume after it. What varies is only which
element the dit becomes:

     80-184ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
    188-300ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah

The boundary sits at 188ms, not at the 160ms where the second dah
begins. The 28ms between the two is send_burst asking the paddles a
second time: whether there is to be an element at all is settled at its
grid start, and what it is only once its sidetone can be heard, so a
tap arriving up to 184ms still catches the element that began at 160ms.
That second reading is what the band either side of a boundary is made
of.

Summary: summary_30wpm_dit-tap.json


keying_30wpm_squeeze_*.png -- a hundred and twenty-five runs
------------------------------------------------------------

Both paddles closed at time zero and both let go together at the moment
named in the file's name, moving 4ms per run from 4ms to 500ms. The
squeeze alternates on a 240ms cycle at this speed, so that is two whole
cycles and a little over: the release falls in every part of one, and
the pattern is then seen to repeat rather than assumed to.

Closed together, the squeeze begins with a dit. Since 026e3ec a squeeze
begins with whichever paddle was closed first, and a dit only where
neither was, which is the case these sets hold to. The dah-first sets
below are the other case.

    release       released during   elements sent
      4-104ms     the first dit     dit dah
    108-264ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
    268-344ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
    348-500ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit

Each band ends exactly where the next tone becomes audible: the second
at 104ms, the third at 264, the fourth at 344. That is the rule below
working as written, and it is why the sweep is cut from the sidetone
and not from the keyed grid.

The 4ms step lands on all three of those instants, so in three runs the
squeeze is let go of at the very moment a tone begins -- a moment the
rule has nothing to say about, the space before it having just run out
and the tone after it not yet having sounded for any length of time at
all. Those three are allowed either answer, and the band they are shown
in above is the one the keyer chose. See squeeze_verdict.

This is the case iambic B exists for. The rule, stated against what the
operator can hear: a squeeze let go of while an element's sidetone is
sounding, or during the space after it, owes exactly one more element --
the opposite of that one -- and nothing after it. The one exception is
the Dit Mem window: a squeeze let go of in the leading Dit Mem % of a dah
and the space after it owes nothing. These sets are run with the window
at 0, where it never applies; see Running them.

Alone among the sets here this one is checked as well as drawn. The
heard timeline is cut at each tone's start, so a window is a tone plus
the space following it and every release falls in one, bar the three
that land on a cut. Each run says which element it was released during,
what was owed for it, and what actually followed, and the run ends with
a count. All of them follow the rule.

Nothing about the check is tied to 30 WPM. There is a second set of it
below at 40, and run besides at 20, 25, 35, 45 and 50 the keyer follows
the rule in every run of every one.

Getting all of them took three things, and the sweep is what said each
of them was needed. It no longer speaks to the second at all, that
having since been made good another way; the paragraph is kept for what
it says about the first.

The memories measure a paddle against the previous element, and they
measure it from when that element was heard, not from when it was
keyed. The hand works to the sidetone: an element is not there to be
answered, nor to be still squeezing through, until it can be heard.
Measured from the keyed start instead, 21 of the 125 go wrong -- the
last handful of every band, 84 to 100ms and again at 244 to 260, 324 to
340 and 480 to 500. Those are the releases falling after the next
element was keyed but before any of it was audible, and each puts an
unasked-for element after it.

That heard instant is where the output callback really placed this
element's edge. Where an edge is never placed at all -- the stream
replaced under it and its queue dropped -- the keyer falls back on the
keyed start plus the delay the last edge it did place was given, and
over this device the two come to the same instant: made to use that
estimate throughout, this set comes out unchanged, every run of it. So
it says nothing either way about taking the placed edge over the
estimate. It said more when the estimate was reckoned from a lag saved
between sessions rather than from what the audio had just done.

A burst's first element is the exception, and is measured from its
keyed start. That element the operator began themselves, by closing the
paddle; it is the only one whose beginning is known to them without
hearing it, so a squeeze let go of before it can be heard has still
been let go of during it. Measuring it like the rest loses all six
releases inside the lag, from 4 to 24ms.

None of this is visible from the keyed grid. previous_elt_ts is the
instant the element was keyed -- send_burst used to read the clock at
the top of its loop, a whole inter-element gap earlier for every
element but a burst's first, which the lag happened to paper over.

The set is here because 6c715bb broke exactly this and no sweep noticed.
That commit required a remembered paddle to have been closed during the
element it is remembered from, and a released squeeze is the opposite --
closed from before the element began and let go part way through it --
so none of them got its element until efd5c5c. Restoring the broken
guard today leaves the swap, tap and dit-tap sweeps byte identical, and
takes this one from 125 to 25. So does every other way of breaking it
that has been tried: nothing but this sweep notices any of them.

Summary: summary_30wpm_squeeze.json


keying_40wpm_squeeze_*.png -- ninety-four runs
-----------------------------------------------

The same squeeze at 40 WPM, where a dit is 30ms and a dah 90. The cycle
is 180ms rather than 240, so the sweep runs from 4ms to 376ms to cover
the same two cycles and a little over -- three quarters of the ground
in three quarters of the time, at the same 4ms step.

    release       released during   elements sent
      4-80ms      the first dit     dit dah
     84-204ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
    208-264ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
    268-376ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit

All ninety-four follow the rule.

It is here because nothing was watching above 30 WPM, and the speed is
not incidental to this keyer. Two of the things the sets here exist to
hold in place are measured against an element's length, and a dit at 40
WPM is a quarter shorter than at 30. A dit's key up is the tightest
edge in the scheme -- it goes into the queue only once the tone can be
heard, so what is left of the dit after the audio's delay is all the
notice it gets -- and it was that margin, not anything visible at 30
WPM, that sidetone_up_edge_margin_seconds was added for.

The tones start at 24, 84, 204, 264 and 384ms, and the 4ms step lands
on three of them. Those three are the boundary runs described above,
and at this speed they do not all fall the same way: 84 is counted as
during the tone beginning, 204 and 264 as during the one ending. Which
way a run comes down is rounding rather than keying, which is why
squeeze_verdict allows either. Before it did, the release at 204ms was
reported as a failure and was not one. It has since changed sides once
more with nothing about the keying changing: 39926dd reckons the
deadline for an element's key down from the two clocks read afresh
rather than from the ones its queued edge was put on, and the
subtraction rounded the other way.

Summary: summary_40wpm_squeeze.json


keying_40wpm_swap_*.png -- sixteen runs
----------------------------------------

The swap at 40 WPM, from 64ms to 124ms. The same two bands as at 30 WPM
and nothing else in them, which is what the set is here to say:

     64-80ms    dit dah dah dah
     84-124ms   dit dit dah dah dah

Summary: summary_40wpm_swap.json


keying_40wpm_dit-tap_*.png -- forty-two runs
---------------------------------------------

The dah held and the dit tapped for 5ms, from 60ms to 224ms.

     60-144ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
    148-224ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah

Exactly one dit in every run, as at 30 WPM. The boundary sits at 148ms,
where the second dah is keyed at 120 and heard at 144: a tap is decided
by the last look at the paddles, and that look waits for the tone. It
did not always. While the key up was given a fixed margin the look was
taken 8ms early here and the boundary stood at 140, and the margin is
the element's own spare now, which at this speed and delay leaves the
look on the tone.

That is the band this set was added for. While the margin was fixed and
a tap was still measured from the heard start rather than from the last
look, three runs of these forty-two and the sixteen below lost the
tapped element altogether -- the operator tapped a paddle and nothing
came out. Nothing at 30 WPM showed it.

Summary: summary_40wpm_dit-tap.json


keying_40wpm_tap_*.png -- sixteen runs
---------------------------------------

The mirror: the dit held throughout and the dah tapped for 5ms, from
64ms to 124ms.

     64-80ms    dit dah dit dit
     84-124ms   dit dit dah dit

A tap is a paddle closed and released inside one element, which is what
the memory exists for, so this is the set the memory guard is answerable
to. At 30 WPM it is run rather than kept, there being nothing in it to
look at: the guard leaves that sweep untouched, all 21 runs, every
element and every KZD and KZU identical to what they were before it.

Summary: summary_40wpm_tap.json


keying_30wpm_dah-first_*.png -- ten runs
-----------------------------------------

The dah paddle closes at time zero and the dit at the moment named in
the file's name, from 4ms to 40ms; both are held, and let go of together
at 300ms. The dit's closing is swept either side of 24ms, where the
first tone can be heard, since that is where what the burst begins with
is settled: the element is started as the paddle closes, and read again
once it can be heard.

Every run goes dah dit dah dit, and is checked to: begun with the paddle
closed first, and alternating from there.

Until 026e3ec a squeeze with nothing before it always began with a dit.
The dah's tone was queued as its paddle closed, but the reading taken
as the tone could be heard found both paddles closed and made the
element a dit. Run against the keyer before it, 4 to 24ms go dit dah dit
dah -- six of the ten -- and 28ms on, where the dit closed after the dah
was heard, go dah dit dah dit.

Summary: summary_30wpm_dah-first.json


keying_40wpm_dah-first_*.png -- eight runs
-------------------------------------------

The same at 40 WPM, the dit's closing swept from 4ms to 32ms. All eight
go dah dit dah dit. Before 026e3ec six of them began with a dit.

Summary: summary_40wpm_dah-first.json


keying_30wpm_stall_*.png -- a hundred and one runs
--------------------------------------------------

An S and then a T, sent through the MIDI path and timed by the device's
own deltas, as a MoMIDI paddle sends them. The dit is held from time
zero to 200ms, and let go of as the S's third dit ends; the dah is
closed at 400ms and let go of at 460. From 190ms the whole process
stalls until the moment named in the file's name, swept from 200ms to
600: every paddle event the device sends in that time reaches the keyer
at the end of it, in order and a tenth of a millisecond apart, and the
keyer's own thread is held too, waking a millisecond after the last of
them. The stall is shaded on the plots.

It is rc27_linux.txt's stall, where the process stood still for some
0.44s as an S ended, and the order is the hardest one: the keyer wakes
to the reading after the S's last dit with everything already standing,
the T included where the stall outlasted its closing. What the K4 should
be told regardless is the S, the 200ms gap the operator left, and the
T, and every run is checked for exactly that. All 101 send it.

Run against the keyer as it was at 3f12782, with the device's timing but
before the readings were taken as of when they were due (b95114d), the
first 50 pass -- stalls ending before the dah is closed -- and all 51
from 400ms on fail the same way: the reading after the S's last dit,
woken with the dah standing, took it for a tap during that dit, and the
T's dah went out as a fourth element of the S with KZD0040. S and T
went out together as V.

The sweep found two faults of its own besides. Stalls ending while the
dah was still held, 404 to 452ms, sent the right elements but told the
K4 the gap as the thread saw it -- the stall's end, not when the dah
closed -- because a burst begun from a paddle standing closed took its
gap from when it was keyed. It now runs to when that paddle closed, or
to where auto spacing held the element off until. And the harness
itself first delivered two events of the same instant in the wrong
order, which a queue never does.

Summary: summary_30wpm_stall.json


keying_40wpm_stall_*.png -- seventy-five runs
----------------------------------------------

The same at 40 WPM, every time scaled by three quarters: the dit let go
of at 150ms, the dah closed at 300 and let go of at 345, the stall from
142.5ms to the moment swept, 152 to 452ms. All 75 send the S, KZD0150
and the T.

Summary: summary_40wpm_stall.json


Running them
------------

    python3 paddle_sweep.py <a copy of k4companion named *.py> <outdir> \
        <tap|dit-tap|swap|squeeze|dah-first|stall> [wpm] [dit-mem-%]

The speed defaults to 30. At any other, the swept moment and the length
of the run are scaled by it rather than left where they were, so that a
sweep covers the same ground at every speed: the ranges above are cut
to elements, not to milliseconds. The step stays at 4ms.

The harness drives the real Keyer over a virtual clock: paddle events
go in through send_paddle_event on the thread a paddle's device would
report on, send_iambic runs on the keyer's thread a hop later, and the
sidetone edges plotted are the frames next_key_edge actually placed.
Nothing about the keyer is reimplemented.

The paddle events go in without a time of their own, so each is timed
on arrival, as a serial paddle's is. A MIDI paddle's events are timed
by the device's own deltas since 3233b2c (see midi_event_timestamp),
and delivered on time the two come to the same instant. The stall sets
are the exception: they go in through midi_message_callback as a MoMIDI
device sends them, the time since its last event carried in the
messages themselves, which is what lets a late delivery keep the time
it happened.

Timers stand still along with the threads, a real one being bound to
the wall clock this has no use for. The command that puts the K4's
sidetone monitor back is sent from one, which is why a summary carries
the mute at a burst's start and nothing that undoes it.

What the harness does not run is mix_sidetone. It calls
note_block_timing and next_key_edge itself, a block at a time, so the
edges plotted are the ones the output callback really placed -- but the
ease that gives back sidetone_lag lives in mix_sidetone and never runs
here. It would have nothing to do if it did: the ease works only in the
silence between characters, and a run is a single burst. Each run
settles its lag at the first key down and keeps it to the end. Nothing
in these sets speaks to the ease either way; what settled its rate was
logs of real sending, where the silences are the operator's own.

Nor does anything here render a sample. The output callback mixes the
tone over what the K4 is sending -- a queue of packets, a buffer of
what has been drawn but not yet played, a fade where that resumes
after a silence, a crossfade under the tone's own rise and fall -- and
none of it runs in these sets, which take the edges and stop. A click
at the end of every element lived in that mix until an operator
reported it, and the change that introduced it left every one of these
plots identical. What the edges say is when a tone began and ended,
which is what the sets are for; what it sounded like is not in them.

Nor is anything here ever late, the stall sets apart. The keyer's
thread wakes on the instant it asked for and every block is filled
exactly a lead ahead of its DAC time, so a race between the two is
never lost. Until 39926dd an element's key down was queued at the start
of the gap before it and taken back where the paddles came to want
nothing, and a take-back that lost the race to the output callback sent
a dit nobody had asked for, heard and on the air. None of these sets
could show that. It was found in logs of real sending under WASAPI in
exclusive mode, and reproduced here only by making the keyer's reading
late on purpose.

The whole process stalling is the exception: rc27_linux.txt caught one,
and the stall sets above make one happen. For some 0.44s nothing ran,
and the paddle events of that time arrived together at the end of it.
Three changes answer it, and none moves a byte of the other sets. The
paddles are timed by the device rather than by arrival (3233b2c). A
reading is of the paddles as they stood when it was due, not when the
stalled thread got to it (b95114d), so the reading after an S's last
dit does not see the dah of the next character as a tap during it -- S
and T were going out together as V -- and a paddle closed and let go of
again while the keyer was still finishing a burst begins the next one
rather than being lost. And an element never sent no longer moves the
gap the next burst's first KZD carries (65edffc): the K4 was told 47ms
between two characters the operator had left 200ms apart. The stall
sets hold them in place.

The sets kept here are run with Dit Mem % at the keyer's own default
of 0 rather than the application's 33, and a fifth argument sets it.
Since 762c88b the only thing it governs is a released squeeze -- a dit
closed during a dah is remembered wherever in the dah it closes -- so
the swap, tap and dit-tap sets come out the same at either. The squeeze
sets do not: at 33 a squeeze let go of in the leading third of a dah and
its gap is owed no dit, which is what the setting is for. 26 of the 125
runs at 30 WPM fall there, 108 to 156ms and 348 to 396, and 19 of the 94
at 40. squeeze_verdict applies the same window, so every run of both
passes at 33 as at 0.

It takes a copy of the program with a .py suffix because it imports it
as a module.
